  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) The collection history cannot be traced with certainty earlier than the 19th century;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) possibly in Rome by 1631, when a version was noted by Arthur Hopton in a letter of July 26 (or August 7?) to the Earl of Arundel as being in the collection of Cardinal Crescenzi. Subsequently mentioned there by Baldinucci in 1681, who speculated that it once belonged to Henry VIII. However, it is not listed in his inventory of 1542 or 1547. (t) Baldinucci also claimed ("Notizie dei Professori" 1845-47, vol. II, pp. 271) that Anne Boleyn threw it out the window saying "Oh, dear, in this panel he seems to be still alive" and that the panel was subsequently picked up by a passer-by and made its way to Rome to the Palazzo de' Crescenzi. Another story mentions that Henry VIII himself threw it out the window when More was hung;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) after the Crescenzi collection, there are several alternative collection histories up until its ownership by the dealer Ferrar in London:;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) Probably Queen Christina of Sweden (d.1689);</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) Livio Odescalchi (1655-1713);</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) Prince Baldassare Erba Odescalchi, 1713;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) sold with the largest and most valuable part of Queen Christina's collection through Pierre Crozat to Philippe, Duke of Orléans, 1721 (inv., British Library, Add. Ms.20390, f.225, no.4; sale contract dated January 14, 1721 in Archivio Odescalchi, Rome, inv. no.V.B.I);</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) probably part of the German, Flemish and Dutch pictures of the Orléans collection sold through the agency of Lord Kinnaird and Messers Morland and Hammorslay in 1792 to Thomas Moore Slade, Chatham, England, for 350,000 francs;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) Sale, Wilson of Pall Mall, London, Orléans Collection, April 1793, lot 11 (to Ferrar, dealer, London);</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) Alternatively, maybe passed from the Crescenzi collections to a member of the Bonelli family;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) thence to Angelo Bonelli, by whom brought to England between 1809 and 1811;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) by 1858, in the possession of Farrer, dealer, London;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) by whom reportedly sold to a Hungarian nobleman;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) All agree that by 1859, the painting was bought by Henry Huth in England;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) by 1881, Mrs. Henry Huth;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) by 1890, Edward Huth;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) M. Knoedler &amp; Co., London;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(u) purchased in 1912 for $267,932.50 by Henry Clay Frick;</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="561" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">(s) his bequest to The Frick Collection, New York (1912.1.77).</subfield>
  </datafield>
